import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Languages as First-Class Values — design detail","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Languages as First-Class Values — design detail","type":"design","date":"2026-09-02T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","refs":["ADR-0041"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/design/0041-languages-as-first-class-values.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/design/0041-languages-as-first-class-values.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/design/0041-languages-as-first-class-values.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0041-languages-as-first-class-values-—-design-detail" tabindex="-1">ADR-0041: Languages as First-Class Values — design detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0041-languages-as-first-class-values-—-design-detail" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0041: Languages as First-Class Values — design detail”">​</a></h1><p>Moved verbatim from ADR-0041 (ADR-0161 size budget). The ADR carries the rules; this page carries the mechanism, tables and rationale.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0039 made kits thin curations of plugins. ADR-0040 made evaluation an <code>Operation</code> served by composable <code>EvalBehavior</code> rules, dissolving the VM subsystem. Together they completed the platform&#39;s stated vision: every front-end stage — lex, parse, typecheck, lint, format, eval — is a <code>RuleAcceptor</code> plugin built from <code>Behavior</code> rules contributed by other plugins. A kit is a curated list of those plugins; a host loads a kit and runs it.</p><p>That architecture made language <strong>components</strong> first-class: a lexer rule, a grammar rule, an eval rule are all values you can contribute, swap, and compose. It did <strong>not</strong> make language <strong>itself</strong> first-class. Three things are still not values:</p><ol><li><p><strong>A whole language is not a handl value.</strong> <code>Kit</code> is a Rust struct (<code>platform/src/kit.rs</code>), <code>KitManifest</code> is a flat <code>Vec&lt;String&gt;</code> of plugin ids (<code>kit-canonical/src/kit_manifest.rs</code>). Neither is reified as a <code>Term</code>/<code>Value</code> in the platform&#39;s value space (<code>platform/src/value.rs</code>). A handl program cannot bind a language, pass it to a function, store it in a list, or build one at runtime.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>eval</code> is a host primitive, not a handl function.</strong> The <code>kit</code> capability only mutates a <code>KitRegistry</code> (<code>add</code>/<code>remove</code>/<code>info</code>, <code>plugin-ext-kit/src/lib.rs</code>). There is no <code>eval : Language -&gt; Source -&gt; Value</code> a handl program can call. The interpreter is a fixed wall above the program, invisible and untouchable.</p></li><li><p><strong>Rule instantiation is hardcoded Rust, not data.</strong> <code>kit-canonical</code> turns plugin ids into actual rules through a <code>const</code> table and a <code>match</code> statement (<code>kit-canonical/src/lib.rs</code>, <code>rule_for_plugin_id</code>). Adding a genuinely new grammar rule that isn&#39;t already in <code>GRAMMAR_RULE_PLUGIN_IDS</code> requires touching Rust, not handl.</p></li></ol><h3 id="the-insight" tabindex="-1">The insight <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-insight" aria-label="Permalink to “The insight”">​</a></h3><p>When functions went first-class, the act of calling stopped being a special form and became a value-level operation: <code>apply : (A -&gt; B) -&gt; A -&gt; B</code>. The thing that operates and the thing operated on became the same kind of thing.</p><p>The parallel move for languages: the act of interpreting stops being a fixed host and becomes a value-level operation: <code>eval : Language -&gt; Source -&gt; Value</code>. The language you write in and the language you describe become the same kind of thing.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>first-class functions</th><th>first-class languages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>apply : (A -&gt; B) -&gt; A -&gt; B</code></td><td><code>eval : Language -&gt; Source -&gt; Value</code></td></tr><tr><td>authority lives in the function&#39;s closure</td><td>authority lives in the language&#39;s behaviors</td></tr><tr><td>gate the function, not <code>apply</code></td><td>gate the language, not <code>eval</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>this</code>/<code>self</code> = the receiver</td><td><code>self</code> = the running language</td></tr><tr><td>recursion via self-reference</td><td>self-hosting bootstrap via <code>eval(self, ...)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Y combinator unlocks Turing completeness</td><td>Y-for-languages unlocks a universal host</td></tr><tr><td>pure function = safe to apply anywhere</td><td>pure language = safe to eval anywhere</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="why-eval-is-not-a-security-problem" tabindex="-1">Why <code>eval</code> is not a security problem <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-eval-is-not-a-security-problem" aria-label="Permalink to “Why eval is not a security problem”">​</a></h3><p><code>apply</code> is neutral plumbing. The authority lives in the function value (its closure captures what it can do). You protect by controlling which functions you hand out, not by gating <code>apply</code>. A pure function is safe to apply to anything.</p><p><code>eval</code> is, by the exact same move, neutral plumbing. The authority lives in the language value (its behaviors capture what it can do). You protect by controlling which languages you hand out, not by gating <code>eval</code>. A pure language (no I/O behaviors) is safe to eval on any source.</p><p>The protection toolkit is identical, one level up: capability discipline at the value level, effect types on the operator, pure-by-default, gate the value not the plumbing. If the host never offers an <code>fs-write</code> behavior, no <code>Language</code> value can contain it, and no <code>eval</code> of any source under any language can touch the filesystem. The capability is absent at the source, so it is absent everywhere downstream.</p><h3 id="the-arrow-calculus-solves-the-unpredictable-compute-graph-problem" tabindex="-1">The arrow calculus solves the unpredictable-compute-graph problem <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-arrow-calculus-solves-the-unpredictable-compute-graph-problem" aria-label="Permalink to “The arrow calculus solves the unpredictable-compute-graph problem”">​</a></h3><p>The naive objection to first-class <code>eval</code> is that the compute graph becomes opaque — you can&#39;t know what a source does without running it. This is the same objection monads face vs arrows.</p><p>The resolution is the same. Arrows expose the computation as a structured, inspectable value; monads hide the continuation behind an opaque function. Lifted: a <strong>structured</strong> <code>Language</code> value (grammar as data, behaviors as data) gives a statically recoverable compute graph — compilable, typecheckable, optimizable, inspectable. A <strong>vau</strong> behavior (one that receives the unevaluated expression and decides what evaluation means in place) is the <code>ArrowApply</code> escape: it recovers expressiveness but costs static analysis.</p><p>The design is typed coexistence, lifted from the arrow playbook: structured by default, vau as an explicitly-typed opt-in escape, tracked by the type system. Pure/structured code compiles; vau code interprets. The platform already has the arrow substrate — <code>Operation</code>/<code>Behavior</code>/ <code>RuleAcceptor</code> is exactly &quot;a language is a bag of composable behaviors as data.&quot;</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Make <code>Language</code> a first-class value in handl&#39;s value space, and make <code>eval</code> a handl-level function over it. The host shrinks to a kernel that ships arrow primitives, <code>eval</code>, <code>self</code>, and <code>compile</code>. Everything else — including handl itself — is a <code>Language</code> value built from those primitives.</p><h3 id="_1-language-is-a-handl-level-type" tabindex="-1">1. <code>Language</code> is a handl-level type <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-language-is-a-handl-level-type" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Language is a handl-level type”">​</a></h3><p>A <code>Language</code> is a structured value with three components, all data:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>handl: type.any = language {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  lexer:   lexer.from-tokens([number, string, ident, operator, keyword]),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  grammar: grammar.from-rules([</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    grammar.rule(&quot;value&quot;,  primary-value),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    grammar.rule(&quot;call&quot;,   call-rule),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    grammar.rule(&quot;let&quot;,    let-rule),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    grammar.rule(&quot;match&quot;,  match-rule),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    grammar.rule(&quot;handle&quot;, handle-rule),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ]),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  eval:    eval.from-behaviors([arith, boolean, array, object, call, let, match, handle]),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>};</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This replaces the flat <code>KitManifest { plugins: Vec&lt;String&gt; }</code> with a structured description. The manifest names <em>what</em> the language is; the <code>Language</code> value <em>is</em> what the language is.</p><h3 id="_2-eval-is-a-handl-function" tabindex="-1">2. <code>eval</code> is a handl function <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-eval-is-a-handl-function" aria-label="Permalink to “2. eval is a handl function”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>val: type.any = eval(handl, &quot;1 + 2&quot;);     # 3</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>eval : Language -&gt; Source -&gt; Value</code> is the primary primitive. It is always available — it is plumbing, not a gated capability. The authority lives in the <code>Language</code> value, which is constructed with limited behaviors.</p><h3 id="_3-self-is-the-running-language-handed-to-the-program" tabindex="-1">3. <code>self</code> is the running language, handed to the program <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-self-is-the-running-language-handed-to-the-program" aria-label="Permalink to “3. self is the running language, handed to the program”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>lang: type.any = runtime.self();          # the language currently running me</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>val:  type.any = eval(lang, &quot;1 + 2&quot;);     # self-application</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>self</code> is to languages what a function&#39;s name is to a recursive function: the value captures itself in its own behavior set. This enables metacircularity (a language implements itself) and the self-hosting bootstrap.</p><h3 id="_4-language-combinators-are-handl-functions" tabindex="-1">4. Language combinators are handl functions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-language-combinators-are-handl-functions" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Language combinators are handl functions”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>handl-c:    type.any = extend(handl, line-comments);    # a dialect, as a value</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>json-lang:  type.any = language { ... };</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>merged:     type.any = merge(base, extras);             # value-level composition</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>restricted: type.any = restrict(lang, [&quot;value&quot;, &quot;object&quot;]);</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>extend</code>/<code>merge</code>/<code>restrict</code> are the higher-order combinators, the way <code>compose</code> and <code>partial</code> are for functions. <code>lang-map : Language -&gt; [Source] -&gt; [Value]</code> has the same shape as <code>map : (A -&gt; B) -&gt; [A] -&gt; [B]</code> — the tell that languages are actually first-class.</p><h3 id="_5-the-bootstrap-is-a-compilation-step-not-interpretation" tabindex="-1">5. The bootstrap is a compilation step, not interpretation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-the-bootstrap-is-a-compilation-step-not-interpretation" aria-label="Permalink to “5. The bootstrap is a compilation step, not interpretation”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span># handl-defining-handl.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>handl: type.any = language { lexer: ..., grammar: ..., eval: ... };</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>binary: type.any = compile(handl, &quot;native&quot;);    # the self-hosting compiler</span></span></code></pre></div><p>If <code>Language</code> is data and <code>compile : Language -&gt; Target</code> is a function on that data, then the self-bootstrap is one function application. The Futamura projections — the foundational result of partial evaluation — become ordinary currying: fix <code>lang</code> and leave <code>src</code> dynamic, and you have a compiled program; leave both dynamic, and you have the interpreter itself.</p><h3 id="_6-structured-by-default-vau-as-typed-escape" tabindex="-1">6. Structured by default, vau as typed escape <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-structured-by-default-vau-as-typed-escape" aria-label="Permalink to “6. Structured by default, vau as typed escape”">​</a></h3><p>The structured <code>Language</code> value is the default — grammar as data, behaviors as data, compute graph statically recoverable. This is the arrow substrate the platform already has.</p><p><code>vau</code> is an explicitly-typed escape hatch for behaviors that need to intercept evaluation in place (receive the unevaluated expression and decide what evaluation means). The type system marks vau behaviors; pure/structured code compiles, vau code interprets. You opt in, the way you opt into <code>ArrowApply</code>.</p><h3 id="_7-capability-discipline-at-the-language-level" tabindex="-1">7. Capability discipline at the language level <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-capability-discipline-at-the-language-level" aria-label="Permalink to “7. Capability discipline at the language level”">​</a></h3><p>A <code>Language</code> value is constructed with limited authority — only the behaviors it was built with. Sandboxing is static: inspect the <code>Language</code> value&#39;s behaviors at load time and reject if they touch disallowed capabilities. No runtime sandbox required.</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>mod-lang: type.any = kit.build(&quot;combat-mod.hnl&quot;);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>safe: type.bool = capabilities(mod-lang).subset-of(allowed-mod-caps);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>if not safe then reject else load(mod-lang);</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>One compiler, any language.</strong> <code>compile : Language -&gt; Target</code> compiles any language because every language is the same kind of data.</li><li><strong>Self-hosting bootstrap as a compilation step.</strong> The language is data; the compiler is a function on that data; self-hosting is one function application.</li><li><strong>Futamura projections become currying.</strong> Specializing an interpreter into a compiler is ordinary partial application of <code>eval</code>.</li><li><strong>Cross-language optimization.</strong> Embedded languages share one IR; the optimizer fuses across language boundaries, no FFI, no marshalling.</li><li><strong>Types flow across language boundaries.</strong> If behaviors carry their types as data, <code>eval(lang, src)</code> has a computable result type derived from <code>lang</code>&#39;s behavior signatures.</li><li><strong>Every language gets every tool, for free.</strong> One formatter, one linter, one debugger, one profiler — all parameterized by <code>Language</code>.</li><li><strong>The host shrinks to a kernel.</strong> The binary ships arrow primitives, <code>eval</code>, <code>self</code>, <code>compile</code>. Everything else, including handl, is a <code>Language</code> value built at startup.</li><li><strong>Language-level hot reload.</strong> Replace a <code>Language</code> value in a running system; new evaluations use the new language; old evaluations finish under the old one.</li><li><strong>Multiple language versions, simultaneously.</strong> Two <code>Language</code> values in the same process, same value space, no compatibility layer.</li><li><strong>The language is its own specification.</strong> The <code>Language</code> value is the spec; conformance is <code>eval(thisLanguage, src) == expected</code>. No separate document that can drift.</li><li><strong>Mods without serialization or runtime sandboxing.</strong> A mod and host share a value space (no marshalling); the mod&#39;s <code>Language</code> value is statically verified at load time (no runtime sandbox).</li></ul><h3 id="negative" tabindex="-1">Negative <a class="header-anchor" href="#negative" aria-label="Permalink to “Negative”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Types for <code>eval</code> are hard.</strong> If <code>Language</code> is a value, the result type of <code>eval(lang, src)</code> depends on the <em>value</em> of <code>lang</code>, not its type. Typing this requires the type system to reason about which language is being evaluated — dependent-type territory, or accept <code>type.any</code> escape hatches (a cop-out). This is the deepest open problem.</li><li><strong>Performance for the vau case.</strong> When behaviors are opaque functions (vau), the compiler cannot specialize to a language at compile time. The structured case compiles; the vau case interprets. Partial evaluation / staging is the escape but restricts when <code>self</code> can be a runtime value.</li><li><strong>The meta-circular trap.</strong> If every behavior can be redefined, including the ones that do the redefining, the meaning of any expression depends on the entire stack of active redefinitions. Powerful but austere; the typed-coexistence design mitigates but does not eliminate this.</li><li><strong>Nobody has shipped this fully in a typed, performant production language.</strong> Racket is the closest (languages as modules, <code>#lang</code>) but not runtime first-class. MetaOCaml does first-class staging but not arbitrary languages. Kernel does first-class fexprs but not as a production language. The composable part (which handl&#39;s platform already has) is well-trodden; the fully-first-class part is a research frontier with partial ascents and no complete summit.</li><li><strong>Wide blast radius.</strong> Reifying <code>Language</code> as a handl value, exposing <code>eval</code>/<code>self</code>/<code>compile</code> as handl functions, and replacing the flat <code>KitManifest</code> with a structured description touches the platform, the kit layer, the host, and every tool. This is a multi-phase migration, not a single PR.</li></ul><h2 id="implementation-phases" tabindex="-1">Implementation phases <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementation-phases" aria-label="Permalink to “Implementation phases”">​</a></h2><h3 id="phase-1-—-tooling-parameterized-by-language" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — Tooling parameterized by <code>Language</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-tooling-parameterized-by-language" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — Tooling parameterized by Language”">​</a></h3><p>Lowest risk, immediate value, platform already supports it. Make <code>BasicFormatter</code>, <code>BasicLinter</code>, <code>BasicTypechecker</code>, and the LSP take a <code>Language</code> argument instead of being wired to one canonical kit. One formatter formats any language you have a value for.</p><h3 id="phase-2-—-language-as-a-handl-value-eval-as-a-handl-function" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — <code>Language</code> as a handl value; <code>eval</code> as a handl function <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-language-as-a-handl-value-eval-as-a-handl-function" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — Language as a handl value; eval as a handl function”">​</a></h3><p>Reify <code>Language</code> as a <code>Term</code>/<code>Value</code> in the platform&#39;s value space. Replace the flat <code>KitManifest { plugins: Vec&lt;String&gt; }</code> with a structured description (<code>lexer</code>/<code>grammar</code>/<code>eval</code> sections). Expose <code>eval</code> as a handl-level function. Drive <code>rule_for_plugin_id</code> from data so the canonical kit stops being the single source of which grammar rules exist.</p><h3 id="phase-3-—-self-and-the-self-hosting-bootstrap" tabindex="-1">Phase 3 — <code>self</code> and the self-hosting bootstrap <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-3-—-self-and-the-self-hosting-bootstrap" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 3 — self and the self-hosting bootstrap”">​</a></h3><p>Expose <code>runtime.self()</code> and <code>runtime.source-of()</code>. Ship a <code>handl-defining-handl.hnl</code> that defines <code>handl</code> as a <code>Language</code> value and evals itself under itself. The bootstrap becomes a compilation step via <code>compile : Language -&gt; Target</code>.</p><h3 id="phase-4-—-vau-as-a-typed-escape" tabindex="-1">Phase 4 — <code>vau</code> as a typed escape <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-4-—-vau-as-a-typed-escape" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 4 — vau as a typed escape”">​</a></h3><p>Add the vau form — behaviors that receive the unevaluated expression and decide what evaluation means in place. Track vau behaviors in the type system; pure/structured code compiles, vau code interprets. The <code>ArrowApply</code> escape, opt-in.</p><h3 id="phase-5-—-capability-discipline-at-the-language-level" tabindex="-1">Phase 5 — Capability discipline at the language level <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-5-—-capability-discipline-at-the-language-level" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 5 — Capability discipline at the language level”">​</a></h3><p>Static capability verification of <code>Language</code> values at load time. A <code>Language</code> carries its capability set; <code>eval</code> checks it. The mods application: shared value space, no serialization, no runtime sandbox.</p><h2 id="related-work" tabindex="-1">Related work <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-work" aria-label="Permalink to “Related work”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./0001-generic-node-trait-based-capabilities.html">ADR-0001</a> — Generic Node, Trait-Based Capabilities, and Plugin-Driven Canonical Kit</li><li><a href="./0039-kits-are-curated-plugin-lists.html">ADR-0039</a> — Kits Are Curated Plugin Lists (this ADR makes kits value-returning, not just registry-mutating)</li><li><a href="./0040-vm-is-an-operation-not-a-subsystem.html">ADR-0040</a> — VM is an Operation, Not a Subsystem (this ADR makes that operation a handl-level function over a first-class value)</li><li>3-Lisp — reflective tower</li><li>Kernel (vau/fexprs) — first-class operatives</li><li>MetaOCaml — staged metaprogramming, first-class staging</li><li>Racket <code>#lang</code> — languages as modules (module-level, not runtime)</li><li>Arrow calculus (Lindley, Wadler, Yallop) — static structure with monad-like notation; <code>ArrowApply</code> as the escape</li><li>Futamura projections — specialization of interpreters into compilers</li></ul>`,60)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};